-0.036041259765625, 0.01349639892578125, 0.0231170654296875, 0.0259857177734375, -0.022705078125, 0.00666046142578125, -0.0193939208984375, 0.00917816162109375, -0.033599853515625, -0.004764556884765625, -0.0011730194091796875, -0.0199432373046875, 0.020721435546875, 0.0097808837890625, -0.034423828125, 0.01088714599609375, -0.0072021484375, 0.0037631988525390625, -0.019927978515625, 0.035552978515625, 0.0042266845703125, -0.0157012939453125, -0.0016803741455078125, 0.005901336669921875, 0.0117645263671875, -0.0313720703125, 0.02337646484375, -0.003078460693359375, 0.0206146240234375, -0.0255126953125, -0.016754150390625, 0.00893402099609375, -0.023590087890625, -0.02838134765625, -0.0178680419921875, 0.025909423828125, 0.0262451171875, -0.0010881423950195312, -0.024658203125, -0.0159912109375, 0.023101806640625, 0.0104827880859375, 0.0165557861328125, -0.01416015625, -0.006763458251953125, 0.05780029296875, 0.0009984970092773438, -0.0257110595703125, -0.00931549072265625, 0.0123748779296875, 0.01555633544921875, -0.0011949539184570312, 0.021484375, 0.0328369140625, -0.0250091552734375, 0.014984130859375, -0.011077880859375, -0.017791748046875, -0.018157958984375, 0.037750244140625, 0.01010894775390625, -0.0229949951171875, 0.0305328369140625, 0.01238250732421875, 0.00604248046875, 0.0128631591796875, 0.043212890625, -0.026824951171875, 0.0176239013671875, 0.0050811767578125, -0.0234832763671875, 0.048431396484375, 0.019622802734375, -0.01885986328125, 0.03204345703125, -0.0313720703125, 0.007801055908203125, -0.0179595947265625, -0.0223541259765625, 0.002582550048828125, 0.0545654296875, -0.022857666015625, -0.0035419464111328125, 0.01494598388671875, -0.048065185546875, -0.0025043487548828125, 0.0223846435546875, 0.01421356201171875, 0.00014460086822509766, -0.0021839141845703125, -0.0035247802734375, -0.0012254714965820312, 0.0203704833984375, 0.046875, -0.035308837890625, -0.0160064697265625, 0.038787841796875, 0.031463623046875, 0.02532958984375, 0.024871826171875, -0.006954193115234375, -0.034271240234375, -0.021636962890625, 0.01169586181640625, -0.0282135009765625, 0.0006213188171386719, 0.039703369140625, 0.034759521484375, 0.02392578125, 0.02471923828125, 0.01238250732421875, 0.031280517578125, 0.028778076171875, -0.0526123046875, 0.01390838623046875, 0.0260772705078125, 0.036376953125, 0.00795745849609375, -0.01285552978515625, 0.0260772705078125, -0.0128173828125, -0.0380859375, 0.02142333984375, -0.0007257461547851562, 0.005336761474609375, -0.004909515380859375, -0.0281982421875, -0.0027446746826171875, -0.0018701553344726562, -0.01149749755859375, -0.0300445556640625, 0.006816864013671875, -0.0092010498046875, 0.0237884521484375, 0.0230865478515625, -0.034454345703125, -0.005771636962890625, -0.003231048583984375, 0.004032135009765625, -0.00495147705078125, 0.0272216796875, 0.0212249755859375, -0.02435302734375, 0.0022106170654296875, -0.006023406982421875, 0.0204010009765625, 0.0231475830078125, -0.01515960693359375, -0.0036678314208984375, 0.01251220703125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits (see, e.g., Doran & Lasenby [1] for foundational GA methods, and Hitzer [2] for applications to circuit analysis). However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations that govern a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process fundamental to system analysis and control design. Existing approaches to Laplace transforms in hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions (Zhang [3]) and Clifford algebras (Sprossig [4]), are limited in several ways. Specifically, they often treat only scalar and vector parts, do not provide a unified treatment of all multigrade components, and typically assume commutativity between the Laplace parameter and algebra elements. These limitations prevent the simultaneous transformation of coupled scalar, vector, and higher-grade quantities, and can force practitioners to split physical quantities into separate components, losing geometric relationships and increasing analytical complexity\u2014especially in systems with inherently multivector-valued signals, such as multi-phase circuits or electromagnetic field analysis. Thus, a comprehensive and consistent definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra remains lacking, impeding both theoretical development and practical modeling.\n\n**Our work addresses this gap by introducing a formal, grade-wise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in GA, explicitly preserving geometric relationships and accommodating the algebra\u2019s non-commutative structure.** Unlike previous approaches, our method applies to all grades simultaneously, does not require splitting signals, and is compatible with the full structure of GA.\n\nThe main contributions of this work are as follows:\n1. **Definition**: We introduce a precise, grade-wise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras of signature (p, q), valid for all finite p and q, but with practical focus on p + q \u2264 5 due to computational considerations.\n2. **Properties**: We establish key properties of this transform\u2014including linearity, the shifting theorem, the convolution theorem, and initial/final value theorems in the non-commutative GA context\u2014discussing the extent to which classical properties generalize, providing proof outlines, stating necessary assumptions, and clarifying limitations.\n3. **Comparison with Prior Work**: We clarify the relationship and improvements over previous approaches for quaternions and Clifford algebras, highlighting concrete scenarios where our method succeeds and others fail, summarized in Table 1 with explicit references to technical limitations.\n4. **Practical Example**: We demonstrate the practical benefits of our approach through a concrete example involving RLC circuit analysis, showing how geometric relationships are preserved and how the transform is applied in practice.\n\n---\n\n**1. Definitions and Notational Conventions**\n\nLet \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) be a multivector-valued function, where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q). We define the Laplace transform \\( \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) \\) by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex scalar parameter. The exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is interpreted as a scalar function of \\( t \\), acting as a scalar multiplier on each grade component of \\( f(t) \\). If \\( f(t) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\), then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\langle f(t) \\rangle_k \\, dt.\n\\]\n**This means the Laplace transform is applied independently to each grade; no cross-grade terms are produced by the transform itself.** The geometric structure of the signal is preserved because all grades are transformed together, but the transform does not mix grades\u2014cross-grade interactions only arise from the algebraic structure of \\( f(t) \\), not from the Laplace operation.\n\n**Assumptions and Limitations**:  \n- The function \\( f \\) is assumed to be piecewise continuous on every finite interval in \\( [0, \\infty) \\) and of exponential order, i.e., there exist constants \\( M, \\alpha \\) such that \\( \\|f(t)\\| \\leq M e^{\\alpha t} \\) for all \\( t \\geq 0 \\).\n- The integrals defining the Laplace transform must converge for each grade component.\n- All algebraic operations (addition, scalar multiplication, geometric product) are assumed to be well-defined in \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\).\n- The definition is mathematically valid for all finite p and q, but practical computation is feasible only for p + q \u2264 5 due to exponential growth in algebra dimension.\n\n---\n\n**2. Motivation for Restricting to \\( p + q \\leq 5 \\)**\n\nWhile the grade-wise Laplace transform definition is mathematically valid for any finite-dimensional geometric algebra, we focus on algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is not fundamental, but is motivated by computational tractability: as p + q increases, the dimension of the algebra grows exponentially (specifically, as \\( 2^{p+q} \\)). For example, with p + q = 5, the algebra has 32 dimensions; for p + q = 8, it has 256 dimensions. In practical terms, symbolic manipulation and numerical computation of Laplace transforms for multivector-valued functions become infeasible for higher dimensions due to memory and processing constraints. For instance, simulating a system with a 32-dimensional algebra (such as the spacetime algebra, (1,3), or the Euclidean algebra, (3,0)) is manageable on modern hardware, but higher-dimensional algebras quickly exceed the capabilities of standard computational tools. This restriction thus encompasses the most common physical and engineering applications while ensuring practical feasibility.\n\n---\n\n**3. Ambiguity in Exponential Interpretation: Why Not Multivector \\( s \\)?**\n\nAlternative definitions, such as allowing \\( s \\) to be a general multivector or defining the exponential via the geometric product, introduce significant mathematical ambiguities. Specifically, the geometric product in GA is non-commutative and, for some signatures, non-associative for certain subalgebras. If \\( s \\) is a multivector, the exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is not, in general, uniquely defined, and its action on \\( f(t) \\) can depend on the order of multiplication and the specific grades involved. For example, consider \\( f(t) = \\mathbf{e}_1 \\) and \\( s = \\mathbf{e}_2 \\) in \\( \\mathcal{G}_{2,0} \\):\n\\[\ne^{-st} f(t) = e^{-\\mathbf{e}_2 t} \\mathbf{e}_1 = \\left( \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{(-\\mathbf{e}_2 t)^n}{n!} \\right) \\mathbf{e}_1.\n\\]\nExpanding the first few terms:\n\\[\n= \\mathbf{e}_1 - \\mathbf{e}_2 \\mathbf{e}_1 t + \\frac{(\\mathbf{e}_2)^2 \\mathbf{e}_1 t^2}{2} - \\cdots\n\\]\nSince \\( \\mathbf{e}_2 \\mathbf{e}_1 = -\\mathbf{e}_1 \\mathbf{e}_2 \\), the order of multiplication matters, and the resulting series contains mixed-grade terms whose interpretation depends on the order of multiplication. If \\( f(t) \\) itself is a sum of grades, the ambiguity compounds, and the integral may not be unique or well-defined. These issues arise from the non-commutativity and, in some cases, non-associativity of the geometric product. By restricting \\( s \\) to scalars or complex numbers, \\( e^{-st} \\) remains scalar-valued and commutes with all multivector components, ensuring the transform is well-defined, unique, and grade-preserving.\n\n---\n\n**4. Theoretical Properties of the GA Laplace Transform**\n\nWe summarize the main properties below. In each case, the property holds for each grade independently, provided the relevant limits and integrals exist for each grade component. The proofs follow from the classical Laplace transform, applied grade-wise, and assume that the algebraic operations (addition, scalar multiplication, geometric product) are well-defined for the multivector components.\n\n- **Linearity**: For \\( f, g: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) and scalars \\( \\alpha, \\beta \\),\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal{L}\\{\\alpha f + \\beta g\\}(s) = \\alpha \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s) + \\beta \\mathcal{L}\\{g\\}(s).\n  \\]\n  *Proof outline*: Follows from the linearity of the integral and the grade-wise definition.\n\n- **Shifting Theorem**:\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t-a)u(t-a)\\}(s) = e^{-as} \\mathcal{L}\\{f\\}(s),\n  \\]\n  where"}}